 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "The balance sheet is a table showing the financial position on a given date. It has two sides:\n• Assets (left side): what the business owns. Fixed assets, kept for a long time (land, buildings, equipment, vehicles), come first; then current assets, which change quickly (stock, debtors, bank, cash).\n• Liabilities and owner's equity (right side): where the money came from. Owner's equity (capital) first; then debts: long-term (bank loans) and short-term (creditors).\nThe two totals are always equal: that is why it is called a balance sheet.\nHeading: the name of the business, “Balance sheet as at …” (the date). Amounts in FCFA.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "f": 2000000,
   "s": 600000,
   "c": 400000,
   "k": 2500000,
   "l": 500000
  },
  "problem": "Present a simple balance sheet: equipment {f}, stock {s}, cash {c}, capital {k}, bank loan {l} (FCFA).",
  "steps": [
   "Assets: equipment {f} + stock {s} + cash {c} = {f + s + c}.",
   "Liabilities and equity: capital {k} + bank loan {l} = {k + l}.",
   "Both totals are {f + s + c}: the balance sheet balances."
  ],
  "answer": "Assets {f + s + c} = Capital and liabilities {k + l} FCFA",
  "checks": [
   "f + s + c == k + l"
  ]
 },
